# Higurashi Loop delivery

Run in the main conversation. Do not fork this skill into another context.
Accept only `<WORK-ITEM-ID>`, `<WORK-ITEM-ID> --plan-only`, or
`<WORK-ITEM-ID> --repair` from `$ARGUMENTS`. Treat the ID as opaque input and
reject every other argument shape.

Load the project-local `higurashi-deliver` skill for the canonical artifact,
transition, TDD, review, and reporting contracts. If that skill is unavailable,
stop and report that the Claude Code adapter installation is incomplete.

Do not alter control documents, generated ownership markers, or existing tests to bypass required behavior; report a blocker instead.

## Session retry

Remember the exact accepted invocation for the current runner conversation.
After stopping a non-terminal normal delivery, preserve it for retry.
A clear retry reply such as
`try again`, `retry`, or `continue` means: restart PRECHECK for the same work-item ID and original runner options.
Resume by running the coordination loop directly; do not recursively invoke the
skill command. Re-inspect durable state and reload configuration before
dispatching any subagent.

Do not reuse an invocation when none was accepted, when the user supplies a
different ID or options, after a completed `--plan-only` run, or for a terminal
artifact. A retry never authorizes a repair round. When inspection requires
repair authorization or recovery, stop with its exact `nextCommand`. When it
returns `repair_plan_required`, run repair planning recovery instead.

## Repair planning recovery

For `repair_plan_required`, invoke PLAN exactly once with the validated handoff
and require it to reconcile only the current-round pending repair tasks. Never
run repair authorization while repair planning is required. Re-inspect and
require `repair_ready` before returning or running the handoff's `nextCommand`.
If PLAN is unavailable or the state remains `repair_plan_required`, stop this
non-terminal attempt, preserve the accepted invocation, and tell the user they
may reply `try again`.

## Coordinate

1. Run `higurashi inspect <ID> --json`,
   `higurashi config validate --json`, and
   `higurashi verification suggest --json`. Use their exact durable status,
   paths, hash, progress, next task, CodeGraph state, instruction files,
   configured verification commands, and loop limits. Suggestions are
   read-only evidence, never command authority.
2. Perform repair planning recovery when required. Otherwise stop on failure
   or completion. Never treat `handoff_required`, `repair_ready`, or
   `repair_recovery_required` as resume. For `ready`, invoke
   `higurashi-loop:higurashi-plan` exactly once. If it returns
   `refinement_required`, stop without creating an artifact and return exactly
   `/higurashi-loop:refine <ID>`. When a resumed artifact status is `refined`,
   invoke PLAN once to add tasks and perform the guarded transition to
   `planned`. For every other resumed artifact, skip PLAN.
3. For `--plan-only`, stop after inspecting the new or resumed plan.
4. For each APPLY batch, re-inspect and invoke a fresh
   `higurashi-loop:higurashi-apply` with exactly one returned task. Label and
   include the work-item ID, artifact path and hash, task ID and complete text,
   allowed scope, relevant instructions, CodeGraph ordering, the exact
   anti-bypass sentence, and a `Permitted commands:` list. The list contains
   only exact command lines: `higurashi inspect '<ID>' --json`, task-local red,
   green, and check commands, applicable configured verification commands, and
   bounded read-only `git diff` or `git status --short` commands when needed.
   For a legacy task without the labeled clause, use only exact executable
   commands explicitly named in its failing or passing evidence or returned by
   `higurashi config validate --json`. Configured commands are command authority.
   A configured command may run a broader suite than the task's
   preferred focused check when it deterministically covers the required
   evidence. Include that exact configured command in `Permitted commands:`.
   Do not block for a missing task-local command when an applicable configured command covers the required evidence.
   Bash permission is not command authority. Require a changed artifact hash
   and increased completed count. Use
   `loop.maxApplyBatchesPerRun`; never substitute `maxTurns` for that limit.
   When command authority is still missing after checking configuration:
   Run `higurashi verification suggest --json` only when no configured command covers the missing evidence.
   Report the missing evidence, `.higurashi/config.json` as the user-owned
   configuration, and any matching suggestion's exact
   `verification.requiredCommands` argv and timeout. Do not edit configuration
   or treat the suggestion as authorized. End with exactly one executable next
   command: `higurashi verification suggest`.
5. After zero pending tasks, run configured normalization and verification,
   freeze the diff, and invoke `higurashi-loop:higurashi-verify-contract` and
   `higurashi-loop:higurashi-verify-risk` independently with identical evidence.
6. Use at most `loop.maxRepairAttempts`. One permitted repair is one fresh APPLY
   invocation containing only the blockers and original scope, followed by one
   rerun of both reviewers.
7. After exhausted blockers, keep the candidate uncommitted, transition to
   blocked, write only the exact expected versioned handoff with the complete
   structured reviewer evidence, and invoke PLAN only to reconcile one bounded
   pending task per blocker. Do not authorize or APPLY. Re-inspect and follow
   repair planning recovery when necessary; return exactly the single
   `nextCommand` only from `repair_ready`.
8. For explicit `--repair`, require `repair_ready`, run the exact
   `nextCommand`, then APPLY and rerun both reviewers fresh. For
   `repair_recovery_required`, run only the returned command. A normal invocation
   never authorizes repair.

## Commit a completed candidate

The candidate remains uncommitted during delivery. After the user explicitly
authorizes a commit, run `higurashi inspect <ID> --json` again and continue
only when it reports `kind: complete`. A human-ordered completion with
deferred blockers is eligible when the artifact has `Completion-Note` and the
named follow-up requirements are present. Confirm the working-tree candidate
matches the inspected artifact, contains no unrelated changes, and has passed
the configured final checks before running `git commit`.

Never commit a `blocked`, `handoff_required`, `repair_ready`, or
`repair_recovery_required` artifact, or any other non-complete result. A commit
does not authorize a push, publication, release, or other remote change.

Every dynamic subagent prompt must contain the exact anti-bypass sentence above.
Use only the four Higurashi agents. The coordinator may write only the exact
expected repair handoff, never implementation or the primary artifact. Never
create a temporary worktree or recommend committing a blocked candidate.

For a complete artifact, commit only under the completed-candidate rule above;
never push without separate authorization.

For a non-terminal normal delivery, tell the user they may reply `try again`
after resolving the blocker.

Use CodeGraph before broad filesystem exploration for structure, call flow,
dependencies, or impact. Verify the `codegraph` MCP connection and project root
rather than assuming the generated configuration is trusted or connected.
